Use this page when a term is unfamiliar or when you need to know **where a file belongs, who owns a step, or what a result proves**.

## Start with these concepts

Imagine you change the **Place order** button in My Chicken App. Here is how that change goes from your Mac to a user’s phone.

1. **Find the project.** A repository, or repo, is the project’s files and their change history. A branch is a line of work in that repo. A commit records a version of the files so the team can identify exactly what was built.
2. **Understand what the app does.** The screen displays the button and sends the tap to the ViewModel or state owner. The use case checks whether the order is valid. The repository in the app code sends the request through the app’s data layer. The backend is the server that accepts or rejects the order.
3. **Test the behavior.** Run the app and its tests on your Mac. A unit test can check that an invalid quantity never reaches the ordering service. A device test can install the app, tap the button, and check what the user sees. Coverage shows how much production code the tests ran.
4. **Ask for review.** Open a pull request, or PR, so teammates can review the change. If configured, Bitrise starts the PR workflow. Danger checks the PR rules, and test steps check the code. Open the PR and the Bitrise log to see each result.
5. **Build the app.** Bitrise checks out the selected commit and runs the workflow defined in the shared `bitrise.yml`. A build packages the app into a file such as an APK or IPA. Depending on the workflow, Sauce Labs can test the built app on real devices and NowSecure can assess the binary for security findings.
6. **Deliver and verify.** The approved workflow signs and delivers the candidate as required by the destination. An intended user then checks that the exact version is available. Uploading a build artifact to Bitrise and making the app available to users are separate results.
7. **Monitor the release.** Use the configured Datadog monitoring to investigate crashes, errors, or slow screens. Match what you see to the app version and environment before deciding what to fix.

### Know which file to open

| If you want to know… | Open… |
|---|---|
| What app metadata does my team provide? | `campfile.yml` in the application repository. |
| Which shared workflow template did we select? | The enrollment YAML in `mobile-platform-configuration`. Read `template_id` and `template_version`. |
| What commands does Bitrise run? | The selected version’s `bitrise.yml` in `mobile-platform-configuration-bitrise-template`. Platform Engineering maintains it. |
| Which step ran for my change? | The Bitrise build log for your commit and workflow. |
| Can users access this release? | The configured enterprise catalog or store. Match the exact version and intended audience. |

> **First setup or another code change?** Enrollment connects a new application to Bitrise once. For a feature change like this one, use the existing Bitrise app and its configured triggers.

## Repositories and configuration

| Term | What it means | What to remember |
|---|---|---|
| **Application repository** | The Git repository containing the app source, native projects, tests, and dependency lockfiles. | The CAMP file is the only CI/CD configuration the app team maintains here. |
| **CAMP file** | The app metadata file used by the platform. The examples in these guides use `campfile.yml`. | Follow the approved CAMP schema for your application. |
| **Enrollment request** | A secret-free YAML file in `mobile-platform-configuration` that identifies the app, source repo, branch, template, and access. | The documented path is `Config/Teams/<team>/Bitrise/Apps/<app>.yaml`. Its casing matters. Enrollment is for initial provisioning. |
| **Configuration engine** | The platform automation in `mobile-platform-configuration-code` that processes approved requests. | Vela validates the request before protected apply. Review the validation and apply results separately. |
| **Application starter template** | A template that generates application source files. | Use the generated application directory to build and test your app. |
| **Bitrise workflow template** | A shared, versioned workflow definition in `mobile-platform-configuration-bitrise-template`. | Platform Engineering maintains its `bitrise.yml`. The enrollment request selects it with `template_id` and `template_version`. |

> **Where does `bitrise.yml` go?** Use the versioned file in the shared template repo. The **Workflows** menu in Bitrise shows the configuration applied to the app.

## Architecture and application behavior

| Term | What it means | What to remember |
|---|---|---|
| **MVVM** | Model–View–ViewModel separates screen rendering, presentation state, and application behavior. | The view sends actions and renders state. Business rules belong in the domain or use case. |
| **Use case** | An operation that expresses a business action, such as placing a chicken order. | It can validate the order before calling a repository. |
| **Repository in application code** | A contract or implementation that gives the feature access to data or operations. | In architecture examples, this term refers to a code boundary. In Git instructions, it means the source project. |
| **Redux Toolkit** | The tool used for shared Redux state in React Native. | A slice manages its part of the state. Keep API clients and credentials outside Redux state. |
| **SDK** | A package of APIs and tools for integrating a capability. | Use the approved package version and its documented contract. |
| **Feature flag** | Configuration that enables or disables behavior at runtime. | Access to protected resources still requires authorization. |
| **Circuit breaker** | A mechanism that temporarily stops calls to a failing dependency. | Before retrying an order with an unknown result, confirm whether the original operation completed. |
| **FCM and APNs** | Firebase Cloud Messaging and Apple Push Notification service deliver notifications. | Follow the app's approved provider path. FCM registration tokens and APNs device tokens serve different integrations. |
| **Deep link** | A link that opens a specific destination inside the app. | Validate the link and verify the current user can access the destination. |

## Build and test execution

| Term | What it means | What to remember |
|---|---|---|
| **CI and CD** | Continuous integration checks code changes. Continuous delivery prepares an approved candidate for distribution. | Follow the required checks and approvals for the selected route. |
| **Trigger** | A configured event that starts a run, such as a pull request update or a push. | Check which workflow the event selects. |
| **Pipeline, workflow, and step** | A pipeline connects jobs. A workflow runs ordered operations. A step performs one operation. | Start with the workflow name in the build log, then find the step that produced the result. |
| **Runner** | The machine or environment where a workflow executes. | Each runner needs its own dependencies and inputs. Separate runners need an explicit handoff for files or outputs. |
| **Script step** | A Bitrise step that runs shell commands, often embedded under `inputs → content`. | In the governed setup, Platform Engineering maintains these scripts in the approved template. |
| **Danger** | Automation that applies configured PR rules and posts feedback to GitHub. | Inspect the PR feedback and Bitrise result. Code checks and peer reviews have their own results. |
| **Parallel builds** | Separate build jobs run at the same time. | In the supplied v1.10.0 example, QA and UAT can each launch Android and iOS builds together. |
| **Parallel tests and sharding** | Independent test jobs run concurrently. Sharding divides a suite into groups. | A parallel Android and iOS build does not establish that Jest tests were split across jobs. |
| **Jest, Android JUnit, and XCUITest** | Jest runs JavaScript tests. Android JUnit instrumentation runs on Android devices or emulators. XCUITest runs iOS UI tests. | JUnit XML is a report format. Check which runner actually executed the tests. |
| **Coverage** | How much production code tests exercised, measured by supported counters such as lines or branches. | The requirement is 100% within the defined production scope and supported metrics. Check file scope, fresh reports, and meaningful assertions. |
| **Sauce Labs** | Runs prepared tests on real devices and reports their results. | Match the app and test packages to the run. Verify the completed test result after upload. |
| **NowSecure** | Assesses a built mobile binary and provides findings for the configured security gate. | Inspect the completed assessment for the exact candidate and the decision under the approved severity policy. |
| **MCP** | Model Context Protocol lets an approved AI client use tools exposed by an integration. | Bitrise MCP access and the workflow that builds your app are separate concerns. CI runs committed tests. |

## Signing, delivery, and evidence

| Term | What it means | What to remember |
|---|---|---|
| **Artifact** | A file produced by a build, such as an APK, AAB, IPA, or test report. | Identify the exact source revision and build that produced it. |
| **APK, AAB, and IPA** | APK is an installable Android package. AAB is an Android App Bundle used for Play delivery. IPA is an iOS app package. | Use the package and signing configuration required by the selected destination. |
| **Signing** | Applies and verifies the identity used to distribute an app package. | Platform Engineering manages protected signing bindings. Enterprise distribution may also perform destination signing or policy processing. |
| **QA and UAT** | QA is quality assurance. UAT is user acceptance testing. | Route names identify the intended testing context. The configured workflow determines the destination and audience. |
| **Private artifact upload** | Publishes the output to Bitrise build artifacts. | Verify enterprise catalog or store delivery separately. In the supplied v1.10.0 template, Digital.ai deployment defaults to skipped. |
| **Distribution** | Makes an approved version available through the intended enterprise catalog or store route. | Match the exact version or build number and verify access with an intended user. |
| **Partial failure and reconciliation** | Some steps completed, but another failed or its result is unknown. Reconciliation checks the current state using existing evidence. | Preserve confirmed results. Ask the platform owner to check the destination before repeating an external write. |
| **Observability** | Uses runtime data such as crashes, errors, traces, or performance signals to understand app behavior. | Match Datadog data and symbols to the affected release and environment. |

## Choose your next step

| Where you are now | What to do next |
|---|---|
| **I’ve never run this app.** | Use the Apple Silicon Machine Setup guide, then follow First Local Run. Confirm the development app opens before changing code. |
| **I have a feature to build.** | Follow Architecture and the standards for Android, iOS, or React Native. Use the app’s existing state, networking, and shared foundations. |
| **I have a new app repo and no Bitrise app.** | Prepare the app and CAMP file, then follow the CI/CD enrollment guide. The request selects an approved shared workflow template. |
| **The app is already enrolled and I changed code.** | Run the relevant local tests, open an application PR, and inspect the configured Bitrise checks for that commit. |
| **I need to change how Bitrise builds or tests the app.** | Find the selected template ID and version, then work with Platform Engineering on the shared `bitrise.yml`. |
| **I want Copilot or ChatGPT to draft a test.** | Give the approved tool the expected behavior, permitted context, and existing test conventions. Review and run the draft locally before committing it. |
| **A build passed and I need to confirm delivery.** | Open the configured destination. Match the app identity and exact version or build number, then check access for an intended user. |
| **Users report a problem after release.** | Record the affected app version, environment, and time. Use Datadog to look for matching errors, crashes, or performance changes. |

## Understand what each result proves

Read results for the **same source commit and candidate**. A single run can pass one step and still be waiting for another.

| What you see | What you can say |
|---|---|
| **Vela validation passed** | The enrollment request passed its configured checks. Look for a separate live apply result to confirm provisioning. |
| **Danger passed** | The PR passed the configured Danger rules. Check peer review and code test results separately. |
| **A Bitrise step was skipped** | The step did not run. Check whether the workflow condition and release policy allowed the skip. |
| **Tests passed** | The executed tests passed. Check test discovery, required suites, and any separate coverage threshold. |
| **Sauce Labs packages uploaded** | The packages reached the upload destination. Check the completed device run for the actual test outcome. |
| **NowSecure assessment submitted** | An assessment was requested. Open the completed report and check the severity decision for the same binary. |
| **APK or IPA available in Bitrise** | Bitrise produced and uploaded an artifact. Check the configured catalog or store for distribution. |
| **The exact version is visible to an intended user** | You have evidence of availability for that audience. Record installation and launch separately when required. |
